WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.840
A cut on your finger usually closes in roughly a week or a little longer.

2
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:15.060
Near the tip of an onion root, cells divide, and the root pushes deeper into the

3
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.780
soil. In both places, the living thing adds new cells.

4
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.560
Why add more cells, instead of letting each cell grow bigger?

5
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.660
And how does each new cell get a full set of instructions?

6
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:34.073
Oxygen, food and wastes all cross the cell membrane.

7
00:00:35.313 --> 00:00:36.733
Model a cell as a cube.

8
00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:39.813
This is a geometry model, not a measurement.

9
00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:47.153
A cube with sides of two units has a surface of 24 square units and a volume of

10
00:00:47.153 --> 00:00:48.273
eight cubic units.

11
00:00:49.493 --> 00:00:50.673
Double the sides to four.

12
00:00:51.213 --> 00:00:54.893
The surface grows to 96, the volume to 64.

13
00:00:55.713 --> 00:01:00.313
Surface for each unit of volume falls from 3 to 1.5.

14
00:01:01.273 --> 00:01:04.113
A bigger cell has less membrane for its inside.

15
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:10.133
The cell cycle is the series of stages from a new cell to its division.

16
00:01:10.913 --> 00:01:13.973
Interphase, between divisions, is not a rest.

17
00:01:14.953 --> 00:01:16.733
In G1, the cell grows.

18
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:19.633
In S phase, it copies its DNA.

19
00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:22.733
In G2, it gets ready to divide.

20
00:01:23.773 --> 00:01:29.133
Each copied chromosome is two identical sister chromatids, joined at the centromere.

21
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:32.027
Mitosis divides the nucleus.

22
00:01:32.847 --> 00:01:37.247
In prophase, chromosomes coil up and the nuclear envelope breaks down.

23
00:01:38.027 --> 00:01:43.727
In metaphase, spindle fibers, thin protein fibers, line the chromosomes up across

24
00:01:43.727 --> 00:01:49.547
the middle. In anaphase, sister chromatids separate and are pulled to opposite ends.

25
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:54.367
In telophase, a new nuclear envelope forms around each set.

26
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.500
Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm.

27
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.480
In an animal cell, a ring at the middle pinches the membrane inward, making a

28
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.360
furrow, until the cell splits.

29
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.380
A plant cell has a stiff wall and cannot pinch.

30
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.960
A cell plate grows across the middle and becomes a new wall.

31
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.230
So, why more cells, not bigger ones?

32
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:24.670
One reason is that a bigger cell has less membrane for its inside.

33
00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.370
And how does each new cell get a full copy?

34
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.490
The cell copies its DNA first.

35
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:37.710
Mitosis then shares it out, and the two new cells usually have the same DNA.
